# Lesson 6: a trump lead, student (South) on lead. Re-dealt 2026-11-27 so good defense beats it; West now bids 4S himself.
title: Take away dummy's ruffs
id: L06-08
type: exercise
lesson: 6
concepts: 6.9, 6.1
student: S
board: 8

deal:            # spades  hearts  diamonds  clubs
  N  J2     KT83   7653    K96
  E  KQ64   QJ     AJ84    874
  S  873    A64    KQT9    J52
  W  AT95   9752   2       AQT3

auction:
  W Pass   = Not enough to open
  N Pass   = Not enough to open
  E 1D     = 13+ points, 4+ diamonds
  S Pass   = Not enough to bid
  W 1H     = 6+ points, 4+ hearts
  N Pass   = Nothing to say
  E 1S     = Four spades too
  S Pass   = Nothing to say
  W 4S     = Four spades and enough for game opposite an opening hand
  N Pass   = Nothing to say
  E Pass   = Done
  S Pass   = Nothing to add

auction recap:
  East opened 1♦ and showed four spades too. West, a passed hand, liked
  the spade fit enough to bid the game himself. East is declarer, because
  East bid spades first, and so you, South, will lead to the first trick.

lead planning:
  declarer: 13-18 = East opened 1♦ and rebid 1♠ without jumping: about 13 to 18.
  dummy: 10-12 = West passed at first, then jumped to game once a spade fit appeared: 10 to 12.
  partner: 0-7 = 40, minus your 10, East's 13 or more and West's 10 or more, leaves at most 7.
  remind: Declarer bid diamonds, and your diamonds are strong. Who will win the diamond tricks?
  remind: Dummy has four trumps. What might declarer do with them?

play:
  1   S S3*  W S5  N SJ  E SQ
      ok: S D
      S3 key: A trump lead. Declarer bid diamonds first, and you hold the diamond honors, so declarer has diamond losers that dummy's trumps could ruff. Every trump you lead takes away one ruff.
      S3 not D: The ♦K, top of your sequence, is a good lead too. A trump lead goes straight at declarer's plan, though: ruffing diamonds in dummy.
      S3 not H: West bid hearts, and leading away from your ♥A against a suit contract can hand declarer a trick.
      S3 not C: Clubs are unbid and safe enough, but they don't stop declarer's plan. What will declarer want to do with dummy's trumps?
      note: Declarer wins with the ♠Q.
  2   E DA  S D9  W D2  N D3
      note: Declarer cashes the ♦A, planning to ruff diamonds in dummy.
  3   E C8  S C2  W CT  N CK
      note: First, a club finesse. Partner wins the ♣K...
  4   N S2  E S4  S S7  W S9
      note: ...and leads a second trump. Dummy started with four; now it has two.
  5   W CA  N C6  E C4  S C5
  6   W SA  N H3  E S6  S S8
      note: With too few trumps left in dummy, declarer gives up on the ruffs and draws your last trump.
  7   W CQ  N C9  E C7  S CJ
  8   W C3  N D5  E D4  S H4
      note: Dummy's last club takes care of one diamond loser.
  9   W H9  N H8  E HJ  S HA
      note: Your ♥A.
  10  S DK  W ST  N D6  E D8
      note: Your ♦K forces out dummy's last trump.
  11  W H7  N HK  E HQ  S H6
      note: Partner's ♥K.
  12  N HT  E SK  S DT  W H2
  13  E DJ  S DQ  W H5  N D7
      note: Your ♦Q takes the last trick: down one.

play recap:
  Declarer wanted to ruff diamonds in dummy. Your trump lead, and partner's
  second trump when he won the ♣K, left dummy too few trumps for that, so
  diamond losers stayed behind for your ♦K and ♦Q. Down one. A heart or
  club lead gives declarer time to ruff two diamonds and make ten tricks.
  The ♦K happens to beat it too, but the trump lead went straight at
  declarer's plan. When the auction tells you dummy's trumps will be used
  for ruffing, a trump lead is often the best start.
